          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Why does my ceiling have roller marks after painting?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most roller marks come from a dry edge, a dry roller, too much pressure, the wrong nap or patches that absorb paint differently. Each leaves a different pattern. Identify the type in raking light, fix the cause, then recoat whole sections with a fully loaded roller and a wet edge."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Will roller marks disappear when the paint dries?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Sometimes slightly, as the paint levels, but marks that are visible after the paint has set usually stay. Lap marks and flashing often look worse once fully dry, especially in daylight. If they are still visible a day later, plan on fixing them rather than waiting."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How do you fix lap marks on a ceiling?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Let the paint cure, then recoat the whole ceiling or whole sections from wall to wall. Roll in small sections, always into wet paint, and finish each in one direction. Close windows and turn off fans to give yourself more time. Spot touch-ups rarely blend on a ceiling."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Should I sand a ceiling before repainting roller marks?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Only if you can feel ridges. Run your fingertips across the marks; if they are raised, sand them lightly with a fine sanding sponge or pole sander and wipe off the dust. If the marks are smooth and only a sheen difference, a proper recoat is enough."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What causes flashing on a ceiling?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Flashing usually comes from unprimed patches or touch-ups. Porous spackle and joint compound absorb more paint binder, so the topcoat over them dries duller. Prime every patch before painting, and repaint whole sections instead of touching up small spots."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What roller nap prevents marks on a ceiling?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For smooth drywall, a quality 3/8-inch nap with bevelled ends is the usual choice. It carries plenty of paint without leaving heavy stipple. Use a 1/2-inch nap for light texture. Budget covers with square ends are more likely to leave edge lines."
          }
