    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Roller marks on a ceiling are rarely the fault of the paint alone and almost never random. They come from a handful of specific causes: a dry edge, a dry roller, too much pressure, the wrong nap or patches that soak up paint differently. Identify which one you are looking at and the fix is usually one careful coat, rolled properly.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="identify" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Identify Which Marks You Have
          </h2>

          <p>
            Look at the ceiling in the light where the marks are most visible, usually afternoon daylight from the main window or a work light held low and aimed across the surface. Note the shape. Wide stripes running in bands across the ceiling are usually lap marks. Thin parallel lines, often in pairs, are roller-edge lines. Blotchy dull or shiny patches, often matching where you repaired the ceiling, are flashing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next, notice whether the marks are about sheen or about thickness. Run your fingertips lightly across them. If you can feel ridges, you have a thickness problem: paint built up at the edges of the roller or at overlaps. If the surface feels smooth but looks uneven, it is a sheen problem: parts of the ceiling dried with a slightly different gloss than others.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check the pattern against how you worked. Lap marks follow the boundaries between sections you rolled, so they often form a grid roughly four feet apart. Roller-edge lines follow the direction of your final strokes. Flashing follows your repairs, or the band you brushed along the edges when cutting in, which can show as a picture frame around the room.
          </p>

          <p>
            Knowing which mark you have matters because the fixes differ. A lap mark caused by a dry edge needs a recoat rolled with a wet edge. A ridge from the roller edge needs a light sanding first. Flashing over patches needs primer before the topcoat. Repainting without addressing the cause usually just recreates the same marks.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What you see</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Likely cause</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Feel</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Wide bands in a grid</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lap marks from a dry edge</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Usually smooth</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Thin parallel lines</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roller-edge ridges</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Ridged</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Dull or shiny blotches</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Flashing over patches</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Smooth</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Frame around the room</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cut-in band dried first</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Smooth or slight ridge</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Rough orange-peel look</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Wrong nap or overworked paint</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Textured</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="lap-marks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Lap Marks: When the Edge Dries First
          </h2>

          <p>
            Lap marks happen when you roll fresh paint over an area that has already started to dry. The overlap ends up with a double layer of paint that dries with a different sheen and density from the single layer around it, and it shows as a stripe. On a ceiling, where light rakes across the whole surface, those stripes can be very visible.
          </p>

          <p>
            The usual cause is working too slowly, or in sections too large, for the drying conditions. Flat ceiling paint starts to set within minutes, faster in warm rooms, dry air or with a fan blowing. If you roll a big section, stop to move a ladder or refill, then come back to roll the next section, the edge of the first one may already be tacky.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cutting in the whole room before rolling causes a related problem. By the time the roller reaches the edges, the brushed band has dried, and the overlap between brushed and rolled paint shows as a frame around the ceiling. Cut in and roll one wall's worth at a time instead, so the brushed band is still wet when the roller blends into it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Lap marks are more visible with higher sheens and with cheaper, faster-drying paints. A flat ceiling paint formulated for a longer open time is more forgiving. Our <a href="/best-ceiling-paint/">ceiling paint picks</a> note which products level and blend well, and our guide to the <a href="/ceiling-paint-sheen/">best sheen for ceiling paint</a> explains why flat hides overlaps best.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Always roll into wet paint.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Work in small sections, about four feet square, across the short dimension of the room, and roll each new section into the previous one while it is still wet. Never stop partway across a ceiling.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="roller-lines" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Roller-Edge Lines: Too Much Pressure, Too Little Paint
          </h2>

          <p>
            Roller-edge lines are thin ridges of paint left by the ends of the roller cover. They form when the roller is pressed hard enough that paint squeezes out at the edges, which usually happens when the roller is running dry and you push harder to spread what is left. They are more likely with cheap covers whose ends are not bevelled and with very thick paints.
          </p>

          <p>
            The fix in technique is simple: load the roller fully and often, and let it do the work with light pressure. A properly loaded roller leaves a full, even film without pushing. When it starts to feel dry or drag, reload rather than pressing harder. Most streaky ceilings are painted with a roller carrying too little paint.
          </p>

          <p>
            Roller quality matters. A good cover with bevelled edges is far less likely to leave lines than a budget knit cover with square edges. For smooth ceilings, a 3/8-inch nap is the usual choice; our ceiling paint guide recommends a quality 3/8-inch cover rather than a budget one, and that advice is mostly about avoiding exactly this problem.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finishing technique helps too. After spreading paint in a section, make a final pass with light, overlapping parallel strokes in one direction, ideally toward the main window wall. Lifting the roller gently at the end of each stroke, rather than stopping abruptly, avoids leaving a heavy line where the roller stopped.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Roller Basics</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">3/8 in</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Nap for smooth ceilings</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Full</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Load every time</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">Light</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Pressure on the pole</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">One way</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Final strokes</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="flashing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Flashing: Patches and Edges That Dry Differently
          </h2>

          <p>
            Flashing is when parts of the ceiling dry with a different sheen than the rest. The classic case is over repairs. Fresh spackle and joint compound are far more porous than the painted ceiling around them, so they absorb more of the paint's binder, leaving the topcoat over them duller. In raking light, every patch shows as a dull blotch, even though the color matches perfectly.
          </p>

          <p>
            The fix is to prime patches before painting. A coat of primer seals the porous repair so it absorbs paint the same way as the surrounding ceiling. If you skipped this step and the patches are flashing, prime them now and repaint. Our <a href="/best-primer/">primer guide</a> covers which primers seal patches well, and our <a href="/best-spackle/">spackle picks</a> include lightweight fillers that are easy to sand flush.
          </p>

          <p>
            Flashing also happens with touch-ups. Touching up a small area on a ceiling, especially in anything other than flat, often leaves a visible spot because the extra coat dries with a slightly different sheen. On a ceiling, it is usually better to repaint a full section from edge to edge than to dab at small spots.
          </p>

          <p>
            The cut-in band can flash too. Brushed paint is applied in a different texture and often a slightly thicker film than rolled paint, so it can dry with a different sheen. Feather the brushed band thin, roll as close to the edge as you safely can, and roll into the band while it is still wet so the two textures blend.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">WATCH FOR</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Flashing is a prep problem, not a paint problem.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If every patch shows as a dull spot, more topcoat will not fix it. Prime the patches, let the primer dry, then repaint the whole section so the sheen comes out even.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="stipple" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Stipple and Texture That Looks Wrong
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every roller leaves some texture, called stipple. A light, uniform stipple is normal and can help a ceiling look even, because it diffuses light. Problems start when the stipple is heavy, uneven or different from one area to the next. That usually comes from the wrong nap, overworking the paint or rolling into paint that has started to set.
          </p>

          <p>
            A nap that is too thick for the surface leaves a heavy orange-peel texture on a smooth ceiling. Use a 3/8-inch nap for smooth drywall, 1/2-inch for light texture and thicker naps only for heavy texture. If you used a thick nap on a smooth ceiling and the texture bothers you, a light sanding and a recoat with the right nap will even it out.
          </p>

          <p>
            Overworking the paint, rolling back and forth over the same area many times, causes uneven texture and can pull partly set paint, leaving rough patches. Spread the paint, fill the section and finish with a few light passes, then move on. If you see a thin spot after the paint has begun to set, leave it for the second coat.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mixing applicators also causes visible differences. A ceiling started with one roller cover and finished with another, or partly sprayed and partly rolled, can show distinct zones. Use the same type and nap of cover for the whole ceiling, and if you replace a cover partway through, change it at a natural boundary such as a beam or wall.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Surface</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Nap</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">If stipple is too heavy</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Smooth drywall</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3/8 inch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sand lightly, recoat with 3/8</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Light texture</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1/2 inch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Check you are not overworking</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Heavy texture</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3/4 inch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Usually hidden by texture</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Popcorn</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Thick nap or spray</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Test first; texture may loosen</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="fix" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How to Fix Roller Marks on a Ceiling
          </h2>

          <p>
            Let the paint cure fully before fixing anything. Trying to correct marks while the paint is still soft usually makes them worse. Give it at least the recoat time on the label, and longer in cool or humid conditions. Then inspect the whole ceiling in raking light and mark the problem areas lightly with painter's tape on the wall so you can find them again.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you can feel ridges, sand them first. Use a fine-grit sanding sponge or pole sander and work lightly until the ridges are flush, then wipe away the dust with a damp cloth and let it dry. Painting over ridges just coats them; sanding is the only way to make them disappear.
          </p>

          <p>
            Prime any flashing patches with a sealing primer. Then repaint the whole ceiling, or at least whole sections from wall to wall, with a properly loaded roller and a wet edge. Spot-fixing a ceiling almost never works, because the new paint will flash against the old. Roll the final pass of each section in one direction, parallel to the main window wall.
          </p>

          <p>
            Control the conditions. Close windows near the work area, turn off ceiling fans and HVAC vents blowing across the ceiling and avoid the hottest part of the day. That extends the paint's open time and gives you more time to keep a wet edge. Our full guide on <a href="/how-to-paint-a-ceiling/">how to paint a ceiling</a> covers the rolling method step by step.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE FIX</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Sand, prime, then recoat the whole field.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Ridges get sanded, patches get primed and the ceiling gets one more full coat rolled with a wet edge. Touching up individual marks on a ceiling almost always creates new ones.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="prevent" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Prevention Checklist for the Next Ceiling
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buy paint made for ceilings. Dedicated ceiling paints are flat, thick and formulated to resist spatter and hide flaws. Wall paints in higher sheens show marks much more readily. Our comparison of <a href="/ceiling-paint-vs-wall-paint/">ceiling paint and wall paint</a> explains why. A color-change ceiling paint can also help, because it shows you exactly where you have rolled.
          </p>

          <p>
            Prep properly. Patch, sand and prime every repair, wash greasy or dirty ceilings and set up a work light aimed across the ceiling so you can see problems while the paint is wet. Most marks that show in daylight would have been visible under a raking work light at the time.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use good tools. A quality 3/8-inch cover for smooth ceilings, an extension pole for even pressure and a bucket with a grid so you can reload often. A good angled brush for cutting in lets you keep the band thin and feathered. Our <a href="/best-paint-brushes/">paint brush guide</a> covers what makes a brush hold a clean edge.
          </p>

          <p>
            Work in a steady rhythm. Cut in one wall's worth, roll that section in four-foot squares, always into wet paint, finishing in one direction. Do not stop partway across the ceiling. Plan two moderate coats rather than one heavy one, and inspect after each in raking light before the paint sets.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
