        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Touching Up Individual Marks</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Dabbing paint on a few stripes almost always creates new spots, because the touch-up dries with a different sheen. On a ceiling, repaint whole sections from wall to wall, or the whole ceiling, so the finish comes out even.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Painting Over Ridges Without Sanding</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Another coat over roller-edge ridges only coats the ridges. Sand them flush with a fine sanding sponge or pole sander, wipe off the dust and let it dry before recoating. Otherwise the lines will show just as clearly after the new coat.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Recoating Before the First Coat Has Cured</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Rolling over paint that is dry to the touch but not ready for recoat can lift and tear the film, adding texture problems on top of the original marks. Wait for the label's recoat time, and longer in cool or humid rooms.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Fixing Flashing With More Topcoat</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Dull patches over repairs come from porous filler absorbing the paint. More topcoat rarely evens it out. Prime the patches with a sealing primer, let it dry, then repaint so the whole surface absorbs paint the same way.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Painting With Fans and Windows Open</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Air movement speeds drying and shortens the time you have to keep a wet edge. Close nearby windows and switch off ceiling fans and vents blowing across the ceiling while you roll. Ventilate once the ceiling is finished.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my ceiling have roller marks after painting?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most roller marks come from a dry edge, a dry roller, too much pressure, the wrong nap or patches that absorb paint differently. Each leaves a different pattern. Identify the type in raking light, fix the cause, then recoat whole sections with a fully loaded roller and a wet edge.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will roller marks disappear when the paint dries?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Sometimes slightly, as the paint levels, but marks that are visible after the paint has set usually stay. Lap marks and flashing often look worse once fully dry, especially in daylight. If they are still visible a day later, plan on fixing them rather than waiting.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you fix lap marks on a ceiling?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Let the paint cure, then recoat the whole ceiling or whole sections from wall to wall. Roll in small sections, always into wet paint, and finish each in one direction. Close windows and turn off fans to give yourself more time. Spot touch-ups rarely blend on a ceiling.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I sand a ceiling before repainting roller marks?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if you can feel ridges. Run your fingertips across the marks; if they are raised, sand them lightly with a fine sanding sponge or pole sander and wipe off the dust. If the marks are smooth and only a sheen difference, a proper recoat is enough.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What causes flashing on a ceiling?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Flashing usually comes from unprimed patches or touch-ups. Porous spackle and joint compound absorb more paint binder, so the topcoat over them dries duller. Prime every patch before painting, and repaint whole sections instead of touching up small spots.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What roller nap prevents marks on a ceiling?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For smooth drywall, a quality 3/8-inch nap with bevelled ends is the usual choice. It carries plenty of paint without leaving heavy stipple. Use a 1/2-inch nap for light texture. Budget covers with square ends are more likely to leave edge lines.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does ceiling paint help prevent roller marks?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Dedicated ceiling paints are flat, which hides overlaps and sheen differences better than higher sheens, and many are formulated to level well and resist spatter. Color-change ceiling paints also show where you have rolled, which helps you avoid missed spots and uneven coverage.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

